00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.789 align:start position:0%
 
hello and congratulations  so in preparation for this interview i  kind of went down the rabbit hole of  reading about your focus  on education and also the mfa program  you're a part of made me think of a  question  that i often ask and it's what was a  seemingly silly  question that you were afraid to ask on  one of your very first film sets that  you think  aspiring actors should know more about  yes it's an  easy answer perry easy i never had to  worry about hitting a mark  if on stage you just get in the general  vicinity of the light  but on film you've got to hit your mark  and i was always afraid to look down and  i wondered what the trick was is how to  find your mark  and a very gracious director the very  first tv thing i did he said  just look at the mark right before you  say your line that part we can cut out  and then go for it  look at something on the side it's a  landmark like a chair  or a table and use it as landmark and i  felt so much better he made me feel  at ease and i i still remember that even  now i  when i don't hit my mark sometimes i  think just look at it right before you  start  i like that that's a good one to back  pocket i got another question that i  love asking because i'm  always fascinated to hear about a  director's tell  behind the monitor when they really like  what you're doing or  in this case i guess it would be a zoom  call so what can you see  robert zemeckis do when you know he is  really liking a take you're giving  well first of all you need to know that  i did this movie  during the pandemic in my closet  in new york he's in california they sent  a microphone  a computer and an ipad so that the  animators could watch me because as you  can see  they're gonna i'm an animated person and  they're going to  hopefully be inspired by that for the  character zemeckis can see me i can see  him we can communicate and there was one  thing that we were doing and i  just ad-libbed something and he  started going kinda and i thought oh  he's shaking  is he okay and he goes no no i'm  laughing so  when he shakes i know he's good i admire  him quite a bit so i enjoy that  how do i that man when i first got the  script i thought oh they want me for the  evil witch i'm sure you know and then  he's like no no the mouse i was like  what but the far the part is is i got to  create that part and i got to be  directed by him  and now there's a special relationship  by someone that i admire and that i can  learn from and that i hope to work with  again  on set on an actual set so i can learn  from him some more  that would be incredible i hope that  happens  i want to put up a brief spoiler warning  for our audience because i did want to  ask you about the ending because i am  very curious to know what you thought  about the ending when you first read the  script and you saw that they changed the  story back to the book  as far as the kids staying mice i had  mixed feelings  and by the way there were there were a  couple endings i got  throughout the yeah so it kind of  switched back and forth but you know  i think the message this is getting back  to i want to speak really to my younger  fans i think the message here is and i  think is what we're saying with the film  don't be um by the way you look  don't worry about it lead with your  brain lead with your heart  and the things that make you unique and  different embrace them because that'll  probably be what makes you special  i like that message for people of all  ages  to wrap up here i have to ask you about  wicked because i love the book love the  show and i've been so so eager to see  that movie  but there was a recent setback with the  movie so  i think this will be very meaningful to  fans coming from you in particular  what do you think it is about the value  of retelling that story in the feature  film format that no matter the amount of  snags we encounter in the next couple of  months or years will guarantee  that that story will be seen on the big  screen at some point  perry i think it's an important story  for first of all as a  kid growing up in oklahoma i couldn't  get to broadway  i could we couldn't afford that so i  relied on television and books  and occasionally a tour that might come  through tulsa you know  um so what why not  why not make it a movie and let this  metoo movement  which we were doing before the me too  movement  this story between it wicked has the  themes i  like in a show it has the themes of  friendship  forgiveness and love  and between two women  you know the love story is really  between them too  and that's what i want up on that camera  i want it up on the screens  i want it on television so that people  who can't afford to go see the show can  watch it  i haven't thought about that i like that  spin on it though and i'm  very much here for it probably one way  or the other because i'm obsessed with  the story  thank you so much for your time today  huge congrats on the movie  thank you honey bye

